GOAL: Fix a bug in `padStart` in /luxon/src/impl/util.js (around line 101) so that negative numbers are zero-padded correctly (the sign must be preserved and the digits padded to `n`). This makes `DateTime#toISODate()` correctly pad negative years.

CURRENT CODE:

    export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
      if (input.toString().length < n) {
        return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);
      } else {
        return input.toString();
      }
    }

WHY IT IS WRONG: for input = -50 and n = 4 it computes `("0000" + -50).slice(-4)` = `"0-50"`, i.e. it truncates the sign instead of padding the digits. So `DateTime.fromObject({ year: -50, month: 5, day: 25, zone: "utc" }).toISODate()` returns `"0-50-05-25"` instead of the ISO-8601-correct `"-0050-05-25"`. (`toISODate()` uses the format `yyyy-MM-dd`, and `yyyy` calls `this.num(dt.year, 4)` which, for the default en-US locale, ends up calling `padStart(dt.year, 4)`.)

REQUIRED FIX (replace the whole function body; keep the name, the default parameter and the export):

    export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
      const isNeg = input < 0;
      let padded;
      if (isNeg) {
        padded = "-" + ("" + -input).padStart(n, "0");
      } else {
        padded = ("" + input).padStart(n, "0");
      }
      return padded;
    }

Note: `String.prototype.padStart` exists in this environment (Node 10+). If for some reason it is unavailable, an equivalent implementation that avoids it is acceptable, e.g. `padded = isNeg ? "-" + padStart(-input, n) : (input.toString().length < n ? ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n) : input.toString());` — but the observable behaviour must be identical to the version above.

